---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';

const pageSchema = [
  {
    '@type': 'WebPage',
    '@id': 'https://sitrep.md/vs/backlog-md/#page',
    url: 'https://sitrep.md/vs/backlog-md/',
    name: 'sitrep vs Backlog.md - task board or state layer',
    description:
      'An honest comparison of sitrep and Backlog.md: a git-native task board you and your agents author structured tasks into, versus a passive state layer parsed from the organic markdown your agents already wrote.',
    isPartOf: { '@id': 'https://sitrep.md/#website' },
  },
];

const rows = [
  {
    dim: 'What it is',
    sitrep: 'AI project status dashboard - a passive state layer over existing files',
    other: 'Git-native task manager: CLI plus kanban board, tasks as markdown files in the repo',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Format demanded',
    sitrep: 'None - parses the organic markdown agents already write',
    other: 'Its own task file format; tasks are authored into it',
  },
  {
    dim: 'What it reads',
    sitrep: 'Plans, analyses, decision logs, progress notes - files nobody structured for a tool',
    other: 'Its own tasks and docs',
  },
  {
    dim: 'State surfaced',
    sitrep: 'Status, phases, open decisions, due dates and staleness',
    other: 'Task status on a per-repo board',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Scope',
    sitrep: 'Cross-project - one overview across every repo and workspace',
    other: 'Per-repo',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Writes to your files',
    sitrep: 'Three guarded state operations in the document’s own convention; nothing else, ever',
    other: 'Creates and updates its own task files',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Agent integration',
    sitrep: 'MCP server exposing parsed state, read-only first',
    other: 'MCP server for task management',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Sharing and mobile',
    sitrep: 'Planned: read-only, end-to-end-encrypted mirror and share links',
    other: 'Git-based sharing; local web UI bound to 127.0.0.1, no mobile',
  },
  {
    dim: 'License and price',
    sitrep: 'Commercial desktop app, in development',
    other: 'Free, MIT-licensed open source',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Local-first',
    sitrep: 'Yes - no account, no network, no telemetry by default',
    other: 'Yes - no account, no telemetry',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="sitrep vs Backlog.md - task board or state layer"
  description="Backlog.md is a git-native task board you author structured tasks into; sitrep is an AI project status dashboard parsed from the markdown your agents already wrote. When to choose each, honestly - including when Backlog.md is the better pick."
  schema={pageSchema}
>
  <Nav />

  <main class="mx-auto w-full max-w-5xl px-6">
    <header class="flex flex-col gap-4 pt-16 pb-12 sm:pt-20">
      <p class="text-xs font-medium tracking-widest text-accent uppercase">Comparison</p>
      <h1 class="max-w-3xl text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">
        sitrep vs Backlog.md
      </h1>
      <p class="max-w-prose text-lg text-ink-muted">
        Backlog.md is one of the strongest tools in this space: free,
        open-source, local-first, markdown-native, with an MCP server - the
        same values sitrep holds. The difference is the job. If you want a
        task board, Backlog.md is better than sitrep, because sitrep is not a
        task manager and refuses to become one.
      </p>
      <p class="max-w-prose rounded-md border border-line bg-surface-raised px-4 py-3 text-sm text-ink-muted">
        Disclosure: this page is written by the maker of sitrep. Read the
        framing with that in mind - and note that where the honest answer is
        "use Backlog.md", this page says so.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="difference">
      <h2 id="difference" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">The one-sentence difference</h2>
      <div class="mt-5 flex max-w-prose flex-col gap-4 text-ink-muted">
        <p>
          Backlog.md gives you and your agents a structured task format to
          author into - tasks live as markdown files in the repo, move across
          a kanban board, and travel through git. sitrep is an AI project
          status dashboard: a state layer on top of the markdown your AI
          agents already generate - it authors nothing and asks you to author
          nothing.
        </p>
        <p>
          Put differently: with Backlog.md, the source of truth is tasks you
          created. With sitrep, the source of truth is the plans, decision
          logs and progress notes your agents already wrote - sitrep parses
          status, phases, open decisions and due dates out of them, across
          every project at once.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="table">
      <h2 id="table" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Side by side</h2>
      <div class="mt-8 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[40rem] border-collapse text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-line text-left">
              <th scope="col" class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium"></th>
              <th scope="col" class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium">sitrep</th>
              <th scope="col" class="py-3 font-medium">Backlog.md</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              rows.map(({ dim, sitrep, other }) => (
                <tr class="border-b border-line align-top">
                  <th scope="row" class="py-3 pr-4 text-left font-medium">{dim}</th>
                  <td class="py-3 pr-4 text-ink-muted">{sitrep}</td>
                  <td class="py-3 text-ink-muted">{other}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="choose-backlog">
      <h2 id="choose-backlog" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">When to choose Backlog.md</h2>
      <ul class="mt-6 grid max-w-2xl gap-3 text-ink-muted">
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          You want a structured task board that both you and your agents
          create and update tasks on, inside the repo, versioned by git.
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          Your workflow is one repo at a time, and a per-repo board matches
          how you work.
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          You want free, MIT-licensed open source you can read, fork and
          extend.
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          Explicitly decomposing work into tasks is a discipline you want,
          not a friction you resent.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-prose text-sm text-ink-muted">
        A task manager with its own authored format is on sitrep's
        anti-roadmap permanently - that job is Backlog.md's, and it does it
        well.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="choose-sitrep">
      <h2 id="choose-sitrep" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">When to choose sitrep</h2>
      <ul class="mt-6 grid max-w-2xl gap-3 text-ink-muted">
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          The state of your work already lives in organic markdown - plans,
          analyses, decision docs your agents produced - and authoring a
          parallel task structure would just be a second source of truth to
          maintain.
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          You run several projects and need one overview: what moved, what is
          stalled, what is waiting on a decision - across all of them, newest
          activity first.
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          You care about staleness: which docs claim things the repo has
          since moved past.
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          Re-entry is the pain - opening a cold project after weeks and
          spending the first fifteen minutes reconstructing where things
          stand.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="both">
      <h2 id="both" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Using both</h2>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-prose text-ink-muted">
        These tools are complementary, not substitutes. Backlog.md is an
        authored task layer inside a repo; sitrep is a passive state layer
        across repos. If your agents work Backlog.md tasks and also write
        plans and decision docs, sitrep sits above both - it reads whatever
        markdown is there. Nothing about adopting one forecloses the other,
        and they share the same posture:
        <a href="/security/" class="text-accent underline underline-offset-4 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-accent">local-first, no account, no telemetry</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-prose text-sm text-ink-faint">
        sitrep is in development, launching first on macOS. Local-first -
        your files never leave your machine.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
